INT. REPAIR SHOP - DAY

A cramped clock repair shop. Hundreds of ticking clocks on every wall, none in agreement. A bell on the door. A workbench cluttered with gears.

At the counter, a young woman named PRIYA DESAI, 20s, taps her fingers, glancing at the wall of clocks.

HIRAM (O.S.)
(from the back room)
Be right with you!

PRIYA
Take your time.

A delivery man named ZEKE, brown uniform, 30s, crashes through the door with a huge box and trips on the rug. HIRAM PELL, 60s, in a leather apron and jeweler's loupe, bursts through the beaded curtain with a pocket watch in his fist, just as the box flies. Hiram throws up his arms. Priya ducks. Zeke sprawls across the floor. The box lands on the counter with a crash. A hundred clocks chime at once.

HIRAM
(shouting over it)
My cuckoos!

ZEKE
(shouting back)
Sorry!

Priya covers her ears. Hiram lunges for the box. Zeke scrambles to his feet. They both grab the lid at the same time and tug. It pops open, and a spring-loaded cuckoo bird flies out and hits Priya in the forehead. She yelps. Hiram and Zeke look at her, then at each other, then at the bird. The chimes die away.

PRIYA
(rubbing her head)
I came in to pick up my grandfather's watch.

HIRAM
(simultaneously)
Which watch?

^ZEKE
(simultaneously)
Sign here, please.

PRIYA
(into the silence)
The one that stopped.

Hiram lifts the pocket watch from the bench and holds it up by its chain. It swings. It ticks.

HIRAM
It's running.
